==Norwegian==

===Etymology 1===
Inflected form of {{term|vis|lang=no}}.

====Adjective====
{{head|no|adjective form}}

# {{form of|plural form|vis|lang=no}}

===Etymology 2===
From {{etyl|non|no}} {{term|vísa||a manner of singing|lang=non}}, related to {{etyl|non|no}} {{term|vis|lang=non}}.

====Noun====
{{no-noun-c|vis}}

# [[lyrical|Lyrical]] or [[epic]] [[poem]] with plain content and plain [[melody]].
#* ''En strofisk dikt med strofisk melodi såväl litterärt som musikalisk oftast präglad av en '''viss''' enkelhet i stilen''. Bengt R. Jonsson (Swedish)
#*: A [[stanzaic]] poem with a stanzaic melody both literary and musically ususally characterized by to some degree a simplicity of style.

=====Usage notes=====
The Scandinavian term {{term|vise|lang=no}} is less comprehensive than the English {{term|song}} (Norwegian {{term|sang|lang=no}}), the German {{term|Lied|lang=de}} or the French {{term|chanson|lang=fr}}. The term {{term|sang}} is often citing a collective whereas the {{term|vise}} more commonly refers to an {{term|I}}.

